{"name":"Gateway Pillar","hex":"#b2ac9c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gateway","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A passage that can be closed by use of a gate.","A place regarded as giving access to somewhere."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make available using a gateway, or access point."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gateway"},{"term":"pillar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large post, often used as supporting architecture.","Something resembling such a structure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide with pillars or added strength as if from pillars."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pillar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Column","blocks":[{"text":"A column or pillar in architecture and structural engineering is a structural element that transmits, through compression, the weight of the structure above to other structural elements below. In other words, a column is a compression member. The term column applies especially to a large round support with a capital and a base or pedestal, which is made of stone, or appearing to be so. A small wooden or metal support is typically called a post. Supports with a rectangular or other non-round section are usually called piers. Throughout architectural history, especially in Classical and Rennaisance styles, the column has been central to building design."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/National_Capitol_Columns_-_Washington%2C_D.C..jpg/330px-National_Capitol_Columns_-_Washington%2C_D.C..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Column","wikidata":"Q4817"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg/500px-Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.o